Architecure And Flow of Project

AWS Deployment — Step by Step Theory
Primary deployment target. Corresponds to infra/aws/ in your repo.
Step 1 — IAM user + AWS CLI auth
Create an IAM user (never use root) with programmatic access, then run aws configure locally. This authenticates
your terminal to make API calls on your behalf. No infrastructure is created, so no billing starts here.
Step 2 — Terraform init
Downloads the AWS provider plugin Terraform needs to translate your .tf files into real AWS API calls. Reads nothing
from your account yet, creates nothing. No billing.
Step 3 — Terraform plan
Terraform compares your .tf files against the current state of your AWS account and prints a diff of what it would
create, change, or destroy. Purely read-only against your account. No billing.
Step 4 — VPC + subnets creation
terraform apply begins building the network: a VPC, 2+ public subnets (for load balancers) and 2+ private subnets (for
worker nodes/database) across two Availability Zones, plus route tables. Subnets themselves are free; the NAT
Gateway created alongside them is not.
n BILLING STARTS HERE
Step 5 — NAT Gateway + Elastic IP
Created as part of the VPC module so private-subnet resources can reach the internet. This is billed per hour plus
per-GB data processed from the moment it's created, regardless of whether traffic flows through it.
n BILLING STARTS HERE
Step 6 — IAM roles for EKS
Creates the cluster role (lets the EKS control plane call AWS APIs) and node role (lets worker EC2 instances join the
cluster, pull from ECR, write logs). IAM roles themselves are free to create and keep.
Step 7 — EKS control plane
Creates the actual aws_eks_cluster resource — AWS provisions and manages the API server, etcd, and scheduler
for you. This starts the $0.10/hour control-plane fee the instant it finishes creating, and continues until you delete it.
n BILLING STARTS HERE
Step 8 — EKS managed node group
Launches the EC2 instances (t3.medium by default in your Terraform) that register as Kubernetes worker nodes —
this is what actually runs your pods. Standard EC2 instance-hour billing applies from launch.
n BILLING STARTS HERE
Step 9 — ECR repository
Creates a private Docker registry for your image, with vulnerability scanning and a lifecycle policy to auto-expire old
image tags. Storage is billed per GB-month; a small dev image costs fractions of a cent.
n BILLING STARTS HERE

Step 10 — RDS PostgreSQL instance
Provisions a managed Postgres database inside the private subnets, security-group-restricted to the cluster only.
Billed per instance-hour plus storage from the moment it's created.
n BILLING STARTS HERE
Step 11 — kubectl configuration
aws eks update-kubeconfig fetches connection details for the new cluster and writes them to ~/.kube/config, and IAM
identity is mapped to Kubernetes RBAC so your user can actually issue kubectl commands. No new billing — this just
wires up access to what already exists.
Step 12 — Build & push Docker image
docker build packages the Spring Boot app; docker push uploads it to the ECR repo from Step 9 so the cluster has
something to pull.
Step 13 — Deploy Kubernetes manifests
kubectl apply -f k8s/ creates the Deployment, Service, Ingress, and HPA objects. The scheduler places pods on the
worker nodes from Step 8, pulling the image from Step 9.
Step 14 — terraform destroy
Tears down the cluster, node group, NAT Gateway, RDS instance, and VPC in reverse dependency order. Billing for
each resource stops only once it is actually deleted, not when the destroy command is issued.
